ROUND-4 B1.5 — CLOSED. The remedy is now achievable end to end.
----------------------------------------------------------------
Not taken from the message text; executed.

  lab5: studio INSIDE the repo, real manifest with an automated probatio.
    merge #1 -> refuses, naming tree:84b819e3... as the rebased tree.
    Follow the message verbatim: `git checkout opus/W-900`, then
      `bisellium verify W-900 --studio <lab>/studio --repo <lab>`
      -> exit 0, writes certifies: tree:84b819e3db25c389d5c58a9813afb2c91682a99b
      — byte-identical to what merge computed.
    Retry the merge -> master fast-forwarded to opus/W-900.
  lab6 (pr.required + push, the studio's own flow): same remedy, retry
    exits 0, force-with-lease lands the rebased tip on origin
    (origin opus == local opus == 770cb37).

So the message layer WAS the right place for B1.5: the mismatch was never in
verify's hashing, it was that the message named a command run from the wrong
checkout. `verify --commit <ref>` remains a real, cheaper option for CI and I
would take it as a follow-up, not a blocker — but I asked for a remedy an
operator can carry out, and this one now works. Not papering over anything.

B5.1 (NEW, BLOCKING) — the check reads one ref and hashes another.
-------------------------------------------------------------------
`mergeOpusBranch` reads the opus record off the FILESYSTEM
(readFront(join(resolve(studio), "opera", "<id>.md")), branch.ts:274) — i.e.
whatever branch is checked out right now — and hashes the OPUS BRANCH REF
(sourceTreeHash(repo, excludes, branch), branch.ts:135). When the studio
lives inside the repo (the real layout, and this repo's own), those are two
different copies of the same file, and the verdict follows the checkout
rather than the artifact. Both directions are reachable and both are wrong.

  (a) FAILS OPEN — uncertified work lands. lab8, studio inside the repo,
      strategy rebase. The opus is opened on the trunk BEFORE any gate has
      run, so master's copy reads `probationes: { tests: { status: pending } }`
      with no `certifies` at all. The builder then works on the branch:
      verifies there (certifies tree:1256ad4f...), commits that bookkeeping
      to the branch, and adds ONE MORE commit, "feat: more work, never
      certified". Merge is then run from the trunk, as CLAUDE.md documents:
        master's copy (what merge reads): probationes: { tests: { status: pending } }
        branch's copy:                    certifies: tree:1256ad4f...
        bisellium merge W-900 --studio ... --repo ...
          -> "merged opus/W-900 into master"    exit 0
        master log: feat: more work, never certified
                    bookkeeping: verify on the branch
                    feat: add feature
                    bookkeeping: open W-900
      staleCertificateError found no `tree:` certificate in the copy it read,
      returned undefined, and the uncertified commit landed. This is exactly
      the population behaviour 24 claims to cover, defeated by where the
      operator is standing. Nothing here is operator error: `bisellium
      verify` writes front matter to the checkout it runs in, builders work
      on the opus branch in worktrees (this repo's own model), and `merge`
      is run from the trunk because that is the only place `git branch -D
      opus/<id>` can succeed.

  (b) FAILS CLOSED — a correct PR is refused, and this PR is the instance.
      lab7 is a local clone of this repo with origin removed, opus/W-026 at
      a7d3d50, HEAD on master, state flipped to done as `bisellium done`
      would:
        bisellium merge W-026 --studio <clone>/studio --repo <clone>
          -> "opus/W-026's tree no longer matches its certificate — opus
              W-026's gate "tests" certifies tree:5ab03a0b4156..., which no
              longer matches its current tree (tree:7496efcc...)"   exit 1
      tree:5ab03a0b is a hash from an earlier round that survives only in
      MASTER's copy of studio/opera/W-026.md. The branch's own copy certifies
      tree:7496efcc — correct, current, and verified above. The mechanism
      refuses the very PR that introduces it, on evidence it read from the
      wrong file. Harmless in itself; diagnostic of (a).

  Root cause is one line, not two: read-provenance and hash-provenance
  disagree. Read the opus record from the branch being merged (one `git show
  <branch>:<studio-rel>/opera/<id>.md`, falling back to the disk read when
  the studio resolves outside the repo) and both directions close at once.
  A cheaper partial that closes only (a): treat the ABSENCE of a `tree:`
  certificate on a gate the manifest declares `kind: automated` as a refusal
  rather than a pass. Absence is currently indistinguishable from "nothing
  to check", and that is what lands the work.

  This is the third round on this mechanism and the same class each time:
  round 3 it could not fire; round 4 it fired once then stepped aside for the
  retry; round 5 it fires for the wrong file. What is different, and worth
  saying plainly: the round-4 hole is genuinely gone, and this one needs the
  studio inside the repo to bite — which is precisely the configuration
  round-4 A4.4 flagged as untested and behaviour 22 only half-covers (see
  A5.1).

