Advisories
----------
A5.1  Behaviour 22 is the ONLY behaviour with the studio inside the repo,
      and it exercises exactly one property there (the exclude set). Every
      other certificate behaviour — 18, 19, 23, 24, 25 — uses tmpStudio(),
      outside the repo, where the opus record has exactly one copy and
      B5.1 cannot be seen. The blind spot round 4 named is narrower now but
      still the same blind spot: the real layout is under-tested, and the
      one blocking finding of this round lives in it.

A5.2  The brief's behaviour list is enforced by `opus.red_evidence`, and
      that rule counts lines matching /^\s{0,3}\d+\.\s/ (countBehaviours,
      rules/evidence.ts:106-124). The brief's new entries are written
      "8-9.", "10-16.", "17-21.", "22-25." — none of which match. Verified:
      countBehaviours(studio/briefs/W-026.md) === 7. So the rule checks reds
      01-07 and would not notice a missing red for any of 8-25. Reds 10-25
      do in fact exist, so nothing is actually unproven here — but the
      amendment that answered round-4 A4.3 is prose the enforcement cannot
      read, against the standing rule that process enforcement is a check
      rule, never prose. Either write them one per line, or teach
      countBehaviours ranges.

A5.3  Following the remedy verbatim under a non-PR config leaves you
      standing on the opus branch, which guarantees the branch-delete
      failure path: lab5's successful retry exited 1 with "master
      fast-forwarded to opus/W-900, but could not delete opus/W-900 ...
      switch off it and delete manually", even though everything was
      correct. Under pr.required (lab6) it exits 0, because merge never
      deletes. Worth one clause in the message ("...then retry the merge
      from <trunk>") — which, note, is only safe once B5.1 is fixed, since
      today retrying from the trunk is what reads the wrong file.

A5.4  ADOPTION.md now says merge "checks a recorded `certifies: tree:<hash>`
      against the branch's current tree on every call that would land or
      push it". The second half is true as of this round; the first half is
      not — it checks the certificate recorded in the CURRENT CHECKOUT's
      copy. Fix the sentence together with B5.1, not instead of it.

A5.5  Carried unchanged from round 4, still accepted: A4.1 (pull_after_push
      inert under pr.required, documented), A4.5 (a refused merge leaves the
      branch rebased and, with a remote counterpart, diverged — benign under
      force-with-lease, and now no longer a route to landing).

On the design call — still endorsed
------------------------------------
Refuse rather than re-run gates remains right, and this round's finding does
not touch it: the fix is which ref the certificate is read from, not whether
merge should execute anything. One place runs gates.

Verdict
-------
FAIL, round 5. One blocking finding (B5.1), and it is NOT the round-4 one:
that retry transcript no longer lands, under both the local and the
push+pr.required configurations, and I reproduced it three invocations deep
in each. B1.5's remedy is achievable and I ran it end to end. A4.4's mutation
hole is genuinely closed at the figures the builder reported. Reds 22-25 are
what they are labelled.

What blocks is that the refusal reads the opus record from the working-tree
checkout while hashing the branch ref, so under the real layout a merge run
from the trunk can miss a certificate entirely and land uncertified work
(lab8), and can equally refuse a correct branch on a stale trunk-side copy
(lab7, this PR). One read, changed to come from the branch, closes both.

